artifact.self_contained_problem:
A permutation class is a set C of (finite) permutations that is closed under taking pattern-subpermutations: if σ ∈ C and τ is contained in σ as a pattern, then τ ∈ C. Every permutation class C is determined by its (possibly infinite) basis B(C), the antichain of minimal permutations not in C; the class C is called finitely based if B(C) is finite. For a permutation class C, let c_n denote the number of permutations of length n in C, and define the (upper) growth rate gr(C) = limsup_{n→∞} c_n^{1/n}.

Let Γ denote the set of growth rates of all permutation classes, and let Γ_fb ⊆ Γ denote the set of growth rates of finitely based permutation classes. Balogh, Bollobás and Morris formulated conjectures concerning the structure of Γ; among their predictions are the following:
(i) every real number that is a growth rate of a permutation class is algebraic;
(ii) for every real α, the set Γ ∩ [0, α] is well-ordered (in particular, contains no accumulation points from above and no perfect subsets);
(iii) Γ is countable.

These conjectures are known to be false for general permutation classes. Determine whether the analogues of these conjectures hold when Γ is replaced everywhere by Γ_fb; that is, decide whether every growth rate of a finitely based permutation class is algebraic, whether Γ_fb is well-ordered below every bound, and whether Γ_fb is countable.
